---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="ClickTrail vs GA4 and server-side tagging"
  description="An honest architectural comparison: ClickTrail's owned, deterministic, consent-gated trails versus GA4 client-side collection and server-side tagging."
  canonicalPath="/compare/"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep">/</span><span aria-current="page">Compare</span>
      </nav>
      <p class="kicker">Comparison</p>
      <h1>ClickTrail vs. GA4 and server-side tagging</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        ClickTrail is an attribution engine you host, not a hosted analytics
        service. This page describes architectural differences &mdash; not a
        benchmark. Evaluate against your own requirements before choosing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="compare-wrap">
        <table class="compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col">GA4 (client-side)</th>
              <th scope="col">Server-side tagging</th>
              <th scope="col">ClickTrail</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Data ownership</th>
              <td>Collected by Google; processed under Google's terms</td>
              <td>Your tag server holds data in transit, but most setups still forward to vendors</td>
              <td>First-party storage on your domain; payloads land in systems you control</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Determinism &amp; testability</th>
              <td>Vendor processing pipeline; not replayable</td>
              <td>Tag configuration is testable, but attribution logic stays vendor-side</td>
              <td>Pure functions with golden-fixture replay in CI; classifier changes are major semver</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Consent handling</th>
              <td>Consent Mode signals; collection behavior governed by Google</td>
              <td>Handled per tag/vendor; you configure each downstream consent pass-through</td>
              <td>Consent gate injected by the host; nothing starts or persists without it; denial clears payloads</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Ad-blocker resilience</th>
              <td>Blocked by common filter lists</td>
              <td>Fewer blocks if you proxy first-party, but vendor destinations remain exposed</td>
              <td>No third-party endpoints; optional first-party proxy route keeps collection same-origin</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Cost model</th>
              <td>Free tier; GA360 pricing at scale</td>
              <td>Tag-server infrastructure plus per-vendor forwarding costs</td>
              <td>Open source (MIT); self-hosted; no per-event vendor pricing</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose" style="margin-top: 48px;">
        <h2>Reading this honestly</h2>
        <p>
          These are architectural differences, not quality judgments. GA4 is a
          capable product for aggregate reporting, and server-side tagging is
          the right tool when you need vendor forwarding under your own
          endpoint. ClickTrail exists for a different question: not "how many
          conversions?" but "which click produced this conversion?" &mdash;
          with an answer you can replay, audit, and store yourself.
        </p>
        <p>
          The trade is real: you run the storage and own the pipeline. What
          you get in return is determinism (golden fixtures as the executable
          spec), consent gating that starts nothing before grant, and payloads
          with no third-party endpoints to block.
        </p>
        <p>
          Next steps: <a href="/how-it-works/">see the pipeline</a>, read the
          <a href="/event-contract/">event contract</a>, or check whether your
          stack already has an adapter in the
          <a href="/integrations/">integrations hub</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
